WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.900
Our case requests fifty-eight kilowatts after one supply unit is unavailable.

2
00:00:04.320 --> 00:00:08.420
The remaining units provide sixty kilowatts, but the downstream path is qualified

3
00:00:08.440 --> 00:00:10.400
for fifty-five under the stated conditions.

4
00:00:10.840 --> 00:00:12.720
The requested case is not supported.

5
00:00:13.140 --> 00:00:15.800
The correct review decision is to hold that operating case.

6
00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:19.700
Adding the installed nameplates again doesn't change the downstream constraint.

7
00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:23.340
Nor can the team quietly remove the required loss condition to make

8
00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:24.360
the arithmetic pass.

9
00:00:24.860 --> 00:00:29.500
There are legitimate next options: revise the workload requirement, qualify a suitable

10
00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:34.080
distribution design, or change the architecture and revalidate affected interfaces.

11
00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:37.660
Each changes a stated part of the review and requires evidence.

12
00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:41.420
Pause and identify what else you'd check if the downstream limit were

13
00:00:41.460 --> 00:00:41.820
raised.

14
00:00:42.360 --> 00:00:46.660
Supply and redundancy would still need review, as would thermal conditions, connections

15
00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:48.060
and any required margin.

16
00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:52.100
Solving the current bottleneck doesn't prove that no other constraint remains.

